arns  to play flappy bird and we'll see who  wins this right off the bat everyone  kind of sucks right now a few birds  randomly get through but that's out of  hundreds none of them have figured out  much in the way of how to handle the  openings between the pipes so I'm going  to fast forward a bit because this took  a long time for them to actually learn  to do anything the blue team here is  looking really promising and they just  tit a pipe okay not bad but they have to  start over  again the blue team is starting to kill  it they've got a really high score going  on but the red team is also looking  really good here okay so blue team just  blew their lead but the red team is  going strong and Green Team so far has  shown no promise whatsoever if we fast  forward a bit more  red team has pretty much figured out  flappy bird they're just they're just  destroying it so let's declare them the  winner now I'm going to fast forward  even more because it took a while for  blue team to figure things out but they  eventually did around the one hour and  thirty minutes mark taking them at least  an hour longer to get to the point where  it might just run forever Green Team was  unfortunately pretty hopeless  even with another hour and they were  still fumbling around getting scores of  a few dozen and then randomly hitting a  pipe the problem is that their networks  are so massively complex compared to the  other two the number of parameters that  need to be evolved in the complex green  bird is four times as many as in a  relatively simple red bird and twice as  many as in the blue bird so I guess the  moral of the story is less is more I  have no idea whether or not the Green  Team would have eventually mastered  flappy bird I assume that given enough  time it would have but I didn't let it  keep running forever around the three  hour mark at was enough they were still  fumbling around and hadn't improved  substantially so that's it our a I  learned to play flappy bird using some  simple neural networks and genetic  algorithms you can experiment with the  code if you want maybe try to figure out  the smallest network that stills plays  perfectly or try reducing the inputs I  can see you only feeding in the first  pipe for example or removing other  inputs it's all a pretty hand wavy  whatever works I hope this was easy  enough to follow and if you enjoyed this  video and want to see more like it give  it a like and hit that subscribe button  in the corner also let me know what  you'd like to see in the future and  leave a comment below until next time  cheers everyone
 

